Cogan syndrome
Rheumatology & Immunology
Interstitial keratitis, Sensorineural hearing loss, Vertigo

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-009
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe, debilitating vertigo, profound hearing loss, and excruciating ocular pain preventing mobilization, eating, and basic self-care.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient completely incapacitated by violent vertigo and sudden deafness, complicated by severe eye pain. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was paramount to distinguish from a massive posterior circulation stroke, assess for systemic vasculitic compromise, and prescribe immediate vestibular sedatives and steroids, as waiting until the next in-hours period posed unacceptable risks of permanent sensorineural hearing loss and severe dehydration from intractable vomiting.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The whole room is spinning violently, my eyes feel like they are burning, and I suddenly can't hear anything out of my left ear."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a rapid, highly distressing evolution of symptoms over the past 48 hours. Initially experienced unexplained fever and profound fatigue, followed by acute onset of severe ocular pain, red hyperemic conjunctiva, and extreme photophobia. Twelve hours ago, the patient developed a persistent sensation of aural fullness and loud tinnitus. This morning, it progressed to rapid bilateral sensorineural hearing loss (worse on the left) and severe, episodic vertigo causing nausea and inability to stand. Also complains of diffuse arthralgias affecting large joints.
Functional Status: Strictly bedbound. Any head movement triggers violent vertigo and retching.
Social/Safety Concerns: Completely dependent on roommate for fluids; high risk of dehydration and falls.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/75 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 38.1 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient lies perfectly still in a darkened room, eyes tightly closed, clutching an emesis basin.
Physical Exam: HEENT: Gross visual acuity is reduced. Slit-lamp examination not possible at bedside, but direct inspection reveals marked, bilateral, deep red conjunctival hyperemia with severe photophobia and ciliary flush, strongly indicative of interstitial keratitis. Neurological/Vestibular: Prominent horizontal nystagmus present. Gross hearing assessment (finger rub/whisper test) demonstrates profound deficit on the left and moderate deficit on the right. Romberg test and gait assessment deferred due to severe vertigo and vomiting risk. Cardiovascular: Heart sounds dual, regular; peripheral pulses equal. The diagnostic tetrad is completely evident clinically: interstitial keratitis, rapid sensorineural hearing loss, and incapacitating vertigo.
Environmental Observations: Room is dark and quiet; bucket placed next to the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Attempted paracetamol with no relief; unable to keep oral fluids down currently.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cogan syndrome - Status: Exacerbated (Acute vestibulo-ocular crisis)
Differentials:

Posterior Circulation Stroke (Cerebellar) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The overwhelming prominence of severe bilateral ocular inflammation (keratitis) and profound hearing loss, which points away from a pure vascular ischemic event and strongly toward an autoimmune vestibulo-ocular process.

Meniere's Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of acute systemic symptoms (fever, large joint arthralgias) and the defining, severe bilateral ocular keratitis, which is absent in Meniere's.

Vestibular Neuritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The concurrent rapid sensorineural hearing loss and extreme eye pain/inflammation; vestibular neuritis typically spares hearing and vision completely.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Incapacitation and dehydration)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering an acute, profoundly disabling vestibulo-ocular crisis characterized by violently active vertigo, sudden deafness, and severe eye inflammation. Given the risk of permanent sensorineural hearing loss and the absolute inability to maintain hydration, urgent after-hours assessment was mandated. The clinical presentation necessitated immediate differentiation from a posterior stroke and the instigation of emergency pathways for high-dose systemic steroids to salvage hearing and vision. Leaving this patient unassessed until standard hours would constitute medical negligence, explicitly validating the MBS 585-600 service.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administer intramuscular vestibular sedative/antiemetic (e.g., Prochlorperazine) immediately to control the violent vertigo and allow safe transport. Urgent referral and immediate transfer to the Emergency Department are required for specialized ophthalmological review (slit-lamp to confirm non-syphilitic interstitial keratitis), urgent audiometry, and initiation of high-dose systemic corticosteroids (and potentially immunosuppressants) to prevent permanent deafness and blindness.
Positive Behavioral Change: Positional discipline: Instruct the patient to fixate their gaze on a single stationary object on the ceiling when lying flat; executing this visual anchoring technique immediately reduces the subjective spinning sensation, providing a sense of physical control over the vertigo. Gradual head turning: Counting to five before completing a turn of the head; this slows endolymph flow in the ear, actively preventing the trigger of further vomiting.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None; acute hospital admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Emesis bags provided.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer is recommended due to the severe vertigo and fall risk. Call 000 immediately if there is any sudden severe chest or back pain, as Cogan syndrome carries a risk of acute aortitis.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe combination of eye and inner ear symptoms points to a rare autoimmune condition. Urgent hospital treatment is critical to attempt to save your hearing and clear your vision.
Clinical Course: Hearing loss can become permanent very rapidly if high-dose steroids are not started immediately. Vertigo typically improves slowly over weeks with treatment.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You need an ambulance to take you to the hospital safely. Do not try to stand up. If you feel a sudden, tearing pain in your chest or back, inform the paramedics immediately as this can be a life-threatening complication of your condition.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is aggressively attacking both the clear window of your eye and the balance/hearing organs in your inner ear. The injection given will stop the vomiting, but you need intravenous steroids at the hospital to stop the attack on your hearing.
Actionable Steps: Keep your eyes open and focus on one spot on the ceiling to reduce the spinning. Move your head in very slow, deliberate motions. Wait for the ambulance to transport you.

